%======================================================================
\section{Induced e.m.f.\ and induced current}

Move a magnet into a closed coil: a galvanometer kicks.
Hold the magnet still: reading returns to zero.
Pull the magnet out: kick the other way.

An \textbf{induced e.m.f.}\ appears when:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item the magnetic field through a circuit \textbf{changes}, or
  \item a conductor \textbf{cuts} across magnetic field lines.
\end{enumerate}
An \textbf{induced current} flows only if the circuit is \textbf{complete}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Situation & Induced e.m.f.? & Sustained current? \\
\midrule
Magnet moving into closed coil & yes & yes \\
Magnet at rest inside closed coil & no & no \\
Rod cutting $B$, open circuit & yes & no \\
Rod on closed rails, cutting $B$ & yes & yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Ways to increase induced e.m.f.\ / current:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item move the magnet or wire faster
  \item use a stronger magnet
  \item more turns (or longer wire in the field)
  \item soft-iron core in the coil
\end{enumerate}

\note{A \emph{steady} magnetic field through a coil does \emph{not} induce an e.m.f.
Induction needs \textbf{change} (or relative motion that changes flux).}

