(3) Load current is unsteady d.c.\ (commutator): true.
Key: (1) and (3) only $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Eddy currents}

Changing flux through a bulk conductor (metal plate, core) induces looping currents
called \textbf{eddy currents}.
\begin{itemize}
  \item They dissipate energy as heat ($I^{2}R$).
  \item By Lenz, they oppose the motion that creates them (electromagnetic damping /
        magnetic braking).
  \item Reduced by laminating soft-iron cores (thin sheets insulated from each other).
\end{itemize}

%======================================================================
\section{Summary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Idea & Formula / rule \\
\midrule
Magnetic flux & $\Phi=BA\cos\theta$ \\
Faraday & $\mathcal{E}=N|\Delta\Phi|/\Delta t$ \\
Lenz & induced current opposes the change \\
Motional e.m.f. & $\mathcal{E}=B\ell v$ \\
Generators & slip rings $\to$ a.c.; commutator $\to$ d.c. \\
Eddy currents & heating + damping; reduce by laminating \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\note{Checklist: (1)~Is there a \emph{change} of flux (or cutting of lines)?
(2)~Is the circuit closed (current) or open (e.m.f.\ only)?
(3)~Lenz: what change must the induced current oppose?
(4)~Left hand $=$ force on current; right hand $=$ induced current.}

